 ## Overview
-Sorting-free Pre-integrated Projected Tetrahedra (PBVR) is a volume rendering technique for irregular datasets that eliminates the need for visibility sorting by treating rendering as a stochastic process [S1]. Unlike conventional Projected Tetrahedra which requires back-to-front sorting, and Particle-based Volume Rendering which suffers from visible footprints, PBVR uses pre-integrated projected tetrahedra and controls fragment rendering via opacity-based probability [S1]. It solves the scalability issues of sorting-based methods on large-scale irregular volumes and the quality issues of particle-based methods [S1].
+Sorting-free Pre-integrated Projected Tetrahedra (PBVR) is a sorting-free volume rendering technique for irregular volume datasets that employs projected tetrahedra with pre-integration [S1]. It solves the problem of low image quality in particle-based methods (visible footprints) and the scalability/visibility-sorting requirements of conventional methods by treating volume rendering as a stochastic process where sampling points occur along a viewing ray with probability equal to opacity [S1]. Unlike conventional Projected Tetrahedra, it does not require visibility sorting of tetrahedral cells from back to front [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** An irregular volume dataset represented as a tetrahedral mesh; a transfer function mapping scalar values to color and opacity; a viewing ray or rasterization context.
-*   **Output:** A rendered image where semi-transparency is achieved through stochastic accumulation rather than alpha blending.
+*   **Input:** An irregular volume dataset represented as a set of tetrahedral cells, each associated with a scalar value (density) and color/emissivity. A viewing ray defined by origin and direction.
+*   **Output:** A rendered image pixel value (brightness/color) for each pixel.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-The method operates on projected tetrahedra. Each tetrahedron is projected onto the image plane to form a polygon (triangle or quad). The data structure for a fragment includes its evaluated opacity $\tau$ and luminosity $c$ derived from the density emitter model [S1].
+*   **Tetrahedral Mesh:** A collection of tetrahedra. Each tetrahedron $T_i$ has vertices, a density value $\rho$, and a luminosity/color value $c$.
+*   **Ray Segments:** The viewing ray is subdivided into segments where particle luminosity ($c_k$) and density ($\rho_k$) are constant [S1].
+*   **Pre-integration Table:** A 3D texture or lookup table used for pre-integrated classification, constructed based on the transfer function [S1].
 
 **Core Optical Model:**
-The method uses the density emitter model, assuming the number of particles in a unit volume follows a Poisson distribution [S1].
