 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The basic brightness calculation has a complexity of $O(n^2)$ if intermediate brightness values are considered [S1]. The overall rendering time is proportional to the number of repetitions $N$ and the number of pixels [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is influenced by the image resolution and the number of particles/tetrahedra [S1]. It requires less memory than Hardware-Assisted Visibility Sorting (HAVS), which needs memory space for sorting cell faces about twice that for tetrahedral cells [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On an NVIDIA GeForce 9600GT, approximately 10 million particles (tetrahedra) can be rendered at 25.7 milliseconds per repetition with 1024x1024 resolution [S1]. For 1.4 million tetrahedra, HAVS achieved 1.3 fps, while PBVR's performance scales with pixel count [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** No visibility sorting required; handles large-scale irregular volumes distributed across multiple nodes; avoids artifacts from insufficient k-buffer sizes in HAVS; avoids visible footprints of particle-based methods [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Performance is heavily influenced by the number of pixels; requires multiple repetitions for high quality (increasing time); may struggle with precipitous transfer functions if $N$ is insufficient compared to Sorting-free Projected Tetrahedra (SPT) [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Suitable for large-scale irregular volumes where sorting is impractical or impossible (e.g., distributed computing) and where particle-based methods produce visible artifacts [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance is critical and high repetition counts are prohibitive; when the transfer function has precipitous changes and $N$ cannot be increased sufficiently [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(n^2)$ for basic brightness calculation if intermediate values are considered [S1]. The overall performance is influenced by the number of repetitions $N$ and the number of pixels [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory-consuming as image resolution increases the number of particles [S1]. Avoids the memory overhead of HAVS, which requires memory space for sorting cell faces about twice that for tetrahedral cells [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On NVIDIA GeForce 9600GT, ~10 million particles rendered at 25.7 milliseconds per repetition with 1024x1024 resolution [S1]. On Intel Core 2 Quad 2.83 GHz with Nvidia GeForce GTX 280, PBVR is faster than SPT for large numbers of cells but is greatly influenced by the number of pixels [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Sorting-free, scalable to large-scale irregular volumes distributed across multiple computing nodes, avoids visible footprints of particles when viewpoint is inside the volume, avoids artifacts from insufficient k-buffer size in HAVS [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Low image quality when density changes drastically (insufficient particles), performance heavily dependent on pixel count, requires multiple repetitions for high quality [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** Large-scale irregular volumes where visibility sorting is impractical or impossible (e.g., distributed computing), and when particle footprints are undesirable.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When density functions change precipitously (SPT handles this better), or when memory is extremely constrained and pixel count is high [S1].
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** The method was introduced in the paper "Sorting-free pre-integrated projected tetrahedra" [S1].
-*   **History:** It was developed to address the limitations of Particle-based Volume Rendering (PBVR) which has low image quality (visible footprints) and conventional Projected Tetrahedra which requires visibility sorting [S1]. It builds on the density emitter model and Poisson distribution assumptions for particle counts [S1].
-*   **Usage:** Applied to large-scale irregular volumes from CSM and CFD simulations [S1].
+Sorting-free Pre-integrated Projected Tetrahedra (PBVR) was developed to address the scalability issues of conventional volume rendering techniques that require visibility sorting, which is difficult for large-scale irregular volumes distributed across multiple computing nodes [S1]. It was proposed as an improvement over Particle-based Volume Rendering (PBVR) which suffers from low image quality and visible footprints, and over Projected Tetrahedra (PT) which requires sorting [S1]. The method treats volume rendering as a stochastic process, allowing it to be applied to object-order techniques like cell-by-cell rendering without explicit visibility sorting [S1].
 
